## Authentication

The provider authenticates as a Google Cloud service account, with the
`https://www.googleapis.com/auth/androidpublisher` scope. It looks for
credentials in this order:

1. the `credentials` argument: the JSON text of a service account key;
2. the `GOOGLEPLAY_CREDENTIALS` environment variable: the same JSON text;
3. [application default credentials](https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/application-default-credentials),
   which covers a key file named by `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, `gcloud
   auth application-default login`, and workload identity federation.

`credentials` holds the content of the key, not a path to it. To read a key
from a file, write `credentials = file("key.json")`, or point
`GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS` at the file and leave `credentials` out.

The service account needs two things before any call succeeds:

1. The **Google Play Android Developer API** enabled in the Google Cloud project
   the service account belongs to.
2. An invitation in Play Console, under **Users and permissions**, to the
   service account's email address, with the permissions for what you manage:

   | To manage | The service account needs |
   |---|---|
   | `googleplay_user`, `googleplay_app_grant` | Admin (all permissions) on the account |
   | `googleplay_track`, `googleplay_track_testers` | Release apps to testing tracks, and Manage testing tracks and edit tester lists |
   | `googleplay_subscription`, `googleplay_one_time_product`, `googleplay_converted_region_prices` | Manage store presence |
   | `googleplay_tracks` | View app information |

   Google does not publish which permission each API method checks, so treat
   the table as a starting point. A call the account may not make fails with a
   403 whose reason is shown in the diagnostic.

A permission granted in Play Console can take some time to reach the API.

## Start with an import

The Google Play Developer API cannot create an app. Create the app in Play
Console, then declare what already exists and import it:

```terraform
import {
  to = googleplay_track_testers.internal
  id = "com.example.app/internal"
}

resource "googleplay_track_testers" "internal" {
  package_name  = "com.example.app"
  track         = "internal"
  google_groups = ["developers@example.com"]
}
```

When the configuration matches what is in Play Console, `tofu plan` reports the
import and no change.

